  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A weak SFC low slowly lifting NE along the \nfront from Sern NC will nudge the boundary from the coast toward\nthe I95 corridor. Overall pre- storm environment will be \nsimilar to yesterday: ample instability, support aloft with\nmultiple shortwaves rounding the base of the stout trough aloft\nover NECONUS, and additional bump in effective shear in the\nlower levels thanks to the development of the low to SW. This \nmay provide a marginally better environment for supercell \ndevelopment. If the front does get pulled west as some HiRes \nsolutions exist, the convective risk will also follow suit. The \ngreatest risk of strong to severe thunderstorms remains E of HWY\n17 with damaging winds remaining the primary threat, with hail \npossible in supercells. Because of this, SPC has the bulk of the\narea in some sort of Severe Thunderstorm Risk, coastal zones are\nunder a Slight Risk (lvl 2/5) with Coastal Plain counties under\na Marginal Risk (lvl 1/5). Some thunderstorms will enter coastal\nplain counties early this morning and waning through the morning\nhours. This afternoon, chances increased quickly over the coast\nand slowly progressing inland through the afternoon into the\nevening. The bulk of convection is expected to dissipate after\nsunset, especially after midnight when the last shortwave pushes\noff the coast and SFC low departing to the NE, leading to a less\nconducive flow pattern through the column.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance slowly coming into better agreement\nwith a drier period for TUE and WED, with the low expected to be\nsomewhere offshore near the NC/VA border. However, iso storms\nwill still be possible over Crystal Coast seabreeze locales.\nWED, SFC flow becomes more onshore kicking off a period of\nincreasing moisture and instability across the Southeast U.S. \ncoastline with the potential for another round of unsettled \nweather for late-week.",
